'''Augustus''' (born '''Gaius Octavius'''; 23 September 63&nbsp;BC – 19 August AD&nbsp;14), also known as '''Octavian''' ({{langx|la|Octavianus}}), was the founder of the [[Roman Empire]] and the first [[Roman emperor]] from 27&nbsp;BC until his death in AD&nbsp;14.{{Efn|The dates of his rule are contemporary dates; Augustus lived under two calendars, the [[Roman calendar|Roman Republican]] until 45{{nbsp}}BC and the [[Julian calendar|Julian]] after 45&nbsp;BC. Due to departures from [[Julius Caesar]]'s intentions, Augustus finished restoring the Julian calendar in March AD&nbsp;4 and the correspondence between the [[proleptic Julian calendar]] and the calendar observed in Rome is uncertain before 8&nbsp;BC.{{sfn|Blackburn|Holford-Strevens|2003|pp=670–671}}}} The [[reign of Augustus]] initiated an [[Roman imperial cult|imperial cult]] and an era of [[imperial peace]] (the {{lang|la|[[Pax Romana]]}} or {{lang|la|[[Ara Pacis|Pax Augusta]]}}) when the [[Ancient Rome|Roman world]] was largely free of armed conflict. The [[principate]], a style of government in which the emperor showed nominal deference to [[Roman Senate|the Senate]],{{sfnm|Richardson|2012|1p=225|2a1=Cavalieri|2a2=Assenmaker|2a3=Cavagna|2a4=Engels|2y=2022|2p=18}} was established during his reign and lasted until the [[Crisis of the Third Century]].

Octavian [[Early life of Augustus|was born]] into an [[equites|equestrian branch]] of the [[Octavia gens|{{lang|la|gens|nocat=y}} Octavia]]. His great-uncle, the Roman dictator [[Julius Caesar]], named him as his [[Inheritance law in ancient Rome|primary heir]] in his will; after [[Caesar's assassination]] in 44 BC, Octavian inherited his estate and [[Rise of Augustus|assumed his name]]. He fought for the loyalty of [[Roman army of the late Republic|Caesar's legions]]. He was made a senator during a [[War of Mutina|state emergency]] and seized power by marching on Rome in 43&nbsp;BC, becoming its youngest elected [[Roman consul|consul]]. He, [[Mark Antony]], and [[Marcus Lepidus]] formed [[Second Triumvirate|a triumvirate regime]] with [[Lex Titia|legally sanctioned]] powers to [[proscriptions of the Second Triumvirate|outlaw]] and oppose [[Caesar's assassins and their allies]]. Following their victory at the [[Battle of Philippi]] in 42&nbsp;BC, the triumvirate divided the [[Roman Republic]] among themselves and ruled as ''de facto'' oligarchs. Competing ambitions eventually tore their alliance apart; Octavian had Lepidus exiled in 36&nbsp;BC for [[Bellum Siculum|opposing him]] in [[Roman Sicily|Sicily]], while [[Marcus Agrippa]], Octavian's naval commander, [[War of Actium|defeated Antony]] in [[Roman Greece|Greece]] at the [[Battle of Actium]] in 31&nbsp;BC. Antony and his wife [[Cleopatra]], the [[Ptolemaic dynasty|Ptolemaic]] queen of [[Ptolemaic Kingdom|Egypt]], [[death of Cleopatra|killed themselves]] during [[Octavian's invasion of Egypt]], which then became Octavian's [[Roman Egypt|personal property]].

After the demise of the triumvirate, Augustus reached an accord with the remaining Roman elite: he would restore the façade of a [[Crisis of the Roman Republic|free republic]], centered around the Senate, the [[Roman magistrate|executive magistrates]] and the [[Roman assemblies|legislative assemblies]]. But his [[Political history of the Roman military|control of the military]] and of half of [[Rome's provinces]] meant he maintained [[Legatus Augusti pro praetore|autocratic power]] legitimized by his appointment as [[commander-in-chief]] of most [[Imperial Roman army|Roman armies]]. To avoid the appearance of [[Roman monarchy|monarchy]] or [[Roman dictator|dictatorship]], he eventually refused to stand for reelection to the consulship, but the Senate [[Constitutional reforms of Augustus|granted him the powers]] of the [[tribunate]] and [[Roman censor|censorship]] and the titles {{lang|la|[[princeps]]}} ('first citizen'), {{lang|la|[[Augustus (title)|augustus]]}} ('the revered'), and {{lang|la|[[pater patriae]]}} ({{literally|father of the country}}), and named the month of August after him. After Lepidus died, Augustus also became {{lang|la|[[pontifex maximus]]}} ('supreme pontiff').

Augustus dramatically [[Wars of Augustus|enlarged the Empire]], annexing Egypt, [[Dalmatia (Roman province)|Dalmatia]], [[Pannonia]], [[Noricum]], and [[Raetia]], expanding possessions in [[Africa (Roman province)|Africa]], and completing the conquest of [[Hispania]]. His expansionism, however, suffered [[Battle of the Teutoburg Forest|a major setback]] in [[Germania]]. Beyond the frontiers, he secured the empire with a buffer region of [[client state]]s and negotiated peace treaties with the [[Parthian Empire]] and [[Kingdom of Kush]]. He reformed the Roman system of [[Taxation in ancient Rome|taxation]] and [[Coinage reform of Augustus|currency]], developed [[Roman roads|networks of roads]] with an [[cursus publicus|official courier system]], established a standing professional army, established the [[Praetorian Guard]] as well as official [[cohortes urbanae|police]] and [[vigiles|fire-fighting services]] for the city of Rome, and renovated much of the city during his reign. Augustus was a [[Augustan literature (ancient Rome)|writer]] and [[Patronage in ancient Rome|patron]] of [[Augustan poetry|poets]] such as [[Virgil]], and has been featured in [[Cultural depictions of Augustus|various works of art]] from ancient to modern times. He died in AD&nbsp;14 at age 75 from natural causes, and the Senate posthumously [[Roman deification|deified]] him. Persistent rumors have claimed his wife [[Livia]] poisoned him. He was [[List of Roman emperors|succeeded as emperor]] by his stepson and adoptive son [[Tiberius]].
